  "id": "COMPOUND:3352 - PROTEIN:6340",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that P-Hydroxybenzaldehyde may interact with the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex through multiple indirect pathways. The first pathway links P-Hydroxybenzaldehyde to N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphodiester alpha-N-acetylglucosaminidase, which in turn targets the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex. This indicates a potential regulatory role for P-Hydroxybenzaldehyde in modulating the activity of this complex. The second and third pathways demonstrate that P-Hydroxybenzaldehyde also targets Zinc finger protein GLI1, which is a known target of mometasone and Xanthotoxol, both of which are known to interact with the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex. These findings suggest that P-Hydroxybenzaldehyde may influence the activity of the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex through different molecular mechanisms, possibly by modulating the expression or activity of its target proteins. The evidence is consistent with the possibility that P-Hydroxybenzaldehyde exerts its effects on the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex through a network of downstream targets, highlighting the complexity of the relationship between this compound and the chromatin-modifying complex.",
